{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of non-experts using a checklist will be more reliable on average because the checklist acts as a variance-reduction mechanism that eliminates 'catastrophic omissions' common to single-point failures in intuition.","counterconditions":["The task requires synthesis of variables that cannot be decomposed into a checklist without losing critical emergent properties.","The checklist is poorly designed or contains systemic biases that the non-experts cannot identify."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist transforms the judgment task from a high-dimensional synthesis (expert intuition) into a series of binary, low-variance verifications. While the expert's peak performance is higher, their reliability is undermined by 'availability bias' or 'cognitive tunneling.' The panel's collective adherence to the checklist ensures a higher 'reliability floor' by forcing the consideration of all critical variables, thus shifting the mean reliability upward.","optional_refs":["14aeeb30fbc1c6c2a09fb273769c38612f46c047a029ac512837d77012c8f16f"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A single domain expert will remain more reliable because the checklist is a 'lossy compression' of expertise that fails to capture the conditional logic necessary for complex, verifiable outcomes.","counterconditions":["The task is purely algorithmic or additive in nature.","The expert is suffering from extreme cognitive load or fatigue."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is not a lack of a checklist, but the internalisation of a dynamic, weighted graph of dependencies. A static checklist provides a linear path that non-experts follow blindly. In cases where the 'right' answer depends on the relative weighting of two checklist items (conditional logic), the non-expert panel will follow the checklist to a wrong conclusion, whereas the expert's intuition correctly weights the evidence.","optional_refs":["b0cc47f9abe378ba8ab0c0e5ccb742c001fc81a763c08f779a96a1a62a08b27a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Genuine reliability gains can be distinguished from mere checklist compliance by analyzing the 'error-residue'—the specific nature of the mistakes made when the panel fails.","counterconditions":["The checklist is so comprehensive that no 'outside' errors are possible.","The evaluation set is too small to distinguish error distributions."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the panel is merely complying, errors will occur on items the checklist covers but the panel misinterprets (compliance errors). If there is a genuine reliability gain, the panel's errors will be randomly distributed or limited to items outside the checklist's scope, while their success rate on 'trap' items (which usually trip up intuitive experts) will be significantly higher. A 'reliability gain' is evidenced by the elimination of 'expert-typical' blind spots.","optional_refs":["1a3263ce12db415ffe38545aede15246308d9f5baa9b48cd088549609dd6bb20"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability is a product of 'Wisdom of the Crowd' error-cancellation rather than the checklist's content itself.","counterconditions":["The panel members are not independent (e.g., they discuss and converge on a dominant member's view).","The non-experts share a common systemic bias."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist serves primarily as a coordination mechanism to ensure all panel members are looking at the same data. The actual reliability gain comes from the statistical averaging of independent non-expert judgments, which cancels out idiosyncratic noise. The checklist prevents the panel from diverging too wildly, while the 'crowd' effect pulls the average toward the objective truth.","optional_refs":["f2d684aa78a78ba4778d9aa5993aa8a3f9274dbc976bdd0151829f879b928b49"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"If we treat 'reliability' as the inverse of variance in outcome, then the checklist is essentially a low-pass filter. It removes high-frequency noise (random expert slips) but may also remove high-frequency signal (expert 'hunches' that are actually correct). The 'genuine gain' vs 'compliance' distinction then becomes a question of whether the filter is removing noise or signal. To test this in a single evaluation: introduce a 'decoy' item into the checklist that is irrelevant to the outcome. If the panel marks it as critical to their judgment, they are in 'compliance mode'. If they ignore it or flag it as irrelevant, they are applying a level of critical judgment that suggests genuine reliability.","why_keep_this":"Proposes a concrete experimental method (the decoy item) to solve the distinction problem posed in the prompt."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[]},"simulation_proposals":[]}